MUST-FIX

1. "'`tests/session-create.test.ts:444` (and the matching OpenCode block at `tests/session-create.test.ts:512`) annotate Gemini/OpenCode injection tests as `// AC: @session-creation-and-env-injection ac-inject-fallback`. That AC is specifically for the unknown-harness fallback (`export KSPEC_SESSION_ID=...`), so these annotations misreport coverage for a different behavior.

2. `tests/session-create.test.ts:721` and `tests/session-create.test.ts:744` use `// Task: @add-multi-harness-env-injection` only. Per the project’s AC coverage rules, these need `// AC:` annotations so coverage/validation can attribute them to spec (and trait, if applicable) acceptance criteria. If there is no suitable spec AC for Gemini/OpenCode routing, the spec ACs should be updated rather than using task-only tags.

SHOULD-FIX

1. `src/sessions/store.ts:1740` (`upsertDotenvSessionId`) catches all read errors and treats them as “file missing”. That can mask real read failures (e.g. `EACCES`, `EISDIR`) and may overwrite existing dotenv content in edge cases (e.g. writeable but unreadable file). This should only ignore `ENOENT`, consistent with the JSON injector safety handling.

Notes

- `kspec validate` fails in this repo for pre-existing global reference/alignment/completeness issues (including duplicate `@observations` refs); I did not see PR-specific trait coverage warnings surfaced from the filtered output.
- Targeted tests for the new paths passed locally: `pnpm vitest tests/session-create.test.ts --run -t "Gemini|OpenCode"`.
- The implementation shape (shared dotenv upsert helper + routing branches) looks otherwise reasonable.
